//? Title: Positional Encodings: Rotations, Biases, Context Extension and Coordinates
//? Subtitle: Sinusoidal tables as rotations with offset-only dot products; learned tables; RoPE and its long-range decay bound; ALiBi's attention span in closed form; position interpolation and NTK-aware RoPE scaling; 2-D tables; and Fourier features for a coordinate MLP fitted to the volcano map, using datamunge's nn::sinusoidal_encoding, nn::learned_encoding, nn::apply_rotary, nn::alibi_bias, nn::rope_inverse_frequencies, nn::apply_rotary_at, nn::sinusoidal_encoding_2d, nn::fourier_features and nn::gaussian_fourier_features
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Attention is blind to order, so position must be put in from outside: added to the inputs (sinusoidal or learned tables), rotated into queries and keys (RoPE) or added to the scores (ALiBi). This report verifies the defining property of each to rounding error.
A sinusoidal shift is a fixed rotation, and its dot products equal \(\sum_i\cos(k\omega_i)\). RoPE scores depend only on the offset, also at fractional positions, and obey Su et al.'s long-range decay bound for 200 random pairs. ALiBi's mean attended distance matches \(\sum_k ke^{-sk}/\sum_k e^{-sk}\) to \(3\times10^{-13}\).
New functions stretch RoPE to longer contexts (position interpolation and NTK-aware scaling) and encode continuous coordinates. Position interpolation at \(4m,4n\) reproduces the original scores at \(m,n\) exactly. Gaussian Fourier features estimate the kernel \(e^{-2\pi^2\sigma^2\Delta^2}\) to within about \(1/\sqrt m\).
On the volcano elevation map, a coordinate MLP with raw inputs cannot fit its own training points to better than 1.3 m. Fourier features fit them almost exactly. Test error is best (0.73–0.80 m) with a wide kernel and collapses to worse than predicting the mean once the kernel is narrower than the spacing of the training points.
)

\toc

> The problem

Self-attention computes a weighted average whose weights depend on dot products of content vectors. Permuting the tokens permutes the output in the same way, so without extra information the model cannot tell "dog bites man" from "man bites dog". Positional encodings supply that information in one of three places. They can be added to the token embeddings: a fixed sinusoidal table (Vaswani et al., 2017) or a learned one (Gehring et al., 2017). They can rotate queries and keys by position-dependent angles, as rotary position embedding does (RoPE; Su et al., 2021). Or they can bias the attention scores by distance, as ALiBi does (Press, Smith and Lewis, 2022).
The same idea applies to continuous positions: an MLP that maps pixel coordinates to values needs its inputs encoded to fit fine detail (Mildenhall et al., 2020; Tancik et al., 2020).

This report replaces the original example `examples/nn/0_foundations/0.2_reusable_layer_library/positional_encodings.cpp`. That example printed the sinusoidal table and checked its norms, its shift-as-rotation property and its offset-only dot products. It showed that a learned table receives gradient only at the positions used, checked RoPE against a hand rotation and its relative-position property, and printed ALiBi's biases and the recency preference they induce. All of that is kept.
Added are context extension for RoPE, 2-D tables and coordinate encodings, in `datamunge/nn/positional_variants.hpp`, together with their theory and an application.

> The method

>> Sinusoidal and learned tables

With frequencies \(\omega_i=b^{-2i/d}\) (base \(b=10000\)), the sinusoidal table is \(\mathrm{PE}_{t,2i}=\sin(t\omega_i)\), \(\mathrm{PE}_{t,2i+1}=\cos(t\omega_i)\), with wavelengths from \(2\pi\) to \(2\pi b\). Every row has norm \(\sqrt{d/2}\). By the angle-addition formulas, moving from position \(t\) to \(t+k\) rotates each pair by the angle \(k\omega_i\), a fixed linear map \(R_k\) that does not depend on \(t\). Hence

$$
\mathrm{PE}_{t+k}=R_k\,\mathrm{PE}_t,\qquad \mathrm{PE}_t\cdot\mathrm{PE}_{t+k}=\sum_{i=0}^{d/2-1}\cos(k\omega_i),
$$
\alttext(PE at t plus k equals R k times PE at t, and the dot product of PE t with PE t plus k equals the sum over i of cos of k omega i.)

so similarity depends only on the offset. A learned table is a parameter matrix with one row per position. Only the rows used receive gradient, and positions beyond its length cannot be represented. `nn::sinusoidal_encoding_2d` builds a table for an \(H\times W\) grid of image patches. Half the columns encode the row with a \(d/2\) sinusoidal table and half encode the column. The dot product of two grid positions is then a row-offset term plus a column-offset term.

>> RoPE

RoPE treats each pair \((x_{2j},x_{2j+1})\) of a query or key as a complex number and multiplies it by \(e^{it\theta_j}\) at position \(t\), with \(\theta_j=b^{-2j/d}\). Rotations preserve norms. For a query at \(m\) and a key at \(n\), with \(h_j=q_j\bar k_j\) in complex notation,

$$
\langle R_mq,\,R_nk\rangle=\mathrm{Re}\sum_{j=0}^{d/2-1}h_j\,e^{i(m-n)\theta_j},
$$
\alttext(The inner product of R m q with R n k equals the real part of the sum over j of h j times e to the i times m minus n times theta j.)

which depends on the positions only through \(r=m-n\). Su et al. (2021) bound how this can grow with distance. Abel summation with \(S_j=\sum_{l<j}e^{ir\theta_l}\) and \(h_{d/2}=0\) gives

$$
\big|\langle R_mq,R_nk\rangle\big|\le\max_j|h_{j+1}-h_j|\;\sum_{j=1}^{d/2}|S_j| .
$$
\alttext(The absolute inner product is at most the maximum over j of the absolute difference h j plus 1 minus h j, times the sum over j from 1 to d over 2 of the absolute value of S j.)

The sum \(\sum_j|S_j|\) shrinks as \(r\) grows, because the fast rotations cancel. This is RoPE's built-in preference for nearby tokens.

>> Stretching RoPE to longer contexts

A model trained on contexts of length \(L\) has seen pair \(j\) rotate through angles up to \(L\theta_j\). At length \(sL\) the slow pairs, those with wavelength \(2\pi/\theta_j>L\), reach angles never seen in training. Position interpolation (Chen et al., 2023) divides every rate by \(s\), so position \(st\) has exactly the angles that position \(t\) had. It also compresses the fast pairs, which resolve neighbouring tokens.
NTK-aware scaling (bloc97, 2023; Peng et al., 2024) instead replaces the base \(b\) by \(b\,s^{d/(d-2)}\). Then \(\theta_0=1\) is unchanged and \(\theta_{d/2-1}=b^{-(d-2)/d}/s\) is divided by exactly \(s\), so the slowest pair is interpolated and the fastest is left alone. `nn::rope_inverse_frequencies` returns the rates under either rule, and `nn::apply_rotary_at` rotates by them at any real positions.

>> ALiBi

ALiBi adds \(-s_h(m-n)\) to the score of query \(m\) and key \(n\le m\) in head \(h\), with geometric slopes \(s_h=2^{-8h/H}\). With equal content scores, the last query's weight on the key \(k\) positions back is proportional to \(e^{-s_hk}\), so the mean attended distance is

$$
\bar k_h=\frac{\sum_{k=0}^{T-1}k\,e^{-s_hk}}{\sum_{k=0}^{T-1}e^{-s_hk}}\;\xrightarrow{T\to\infty}\;\frac1{e^{s_h}-1}\approx\frac1{s_h}.
$$
\alttext(The mean attended distance of head h equals the sum over k from 0 to T minus 1 of k e to the minus s h k, over the sum of e to the minus s h k; as T goes to infinity it tends to 1 over e to the s h minus 1, approximately 1 over s h.)

Each head therefore has a span set by its slope, from about one token to hundreds, with no parameters.

>> Fourier features for coordinates

A ReLU MLP on raw coordinates learns low frequencies first and high frequencies very slowly. This is the spectral bias (Rahaman et al., 2019). NeRF's encoding (`nn::fourier_features`) maps each coordinate \(p\) to \((p,\sin(2^k\pi p),\cos(2^k\pi p))_{k<L}\). Random Fourier features (`nn::gaussian_fourier_features`) map \(x\) to \(\gamma(x)=[\cos(2\pi xB),\sin(2\pi xB)]\) with \(B\in\mathbb R^{D\times m}\) drawn \(\mathcal N(0,\sigma^2)\). Then \(\gamma(x)\cdot\gamma(x')=\sum_j\cos(2\pi b_j\cdot(x-x'))\). Its expectation is \(m\) times the characteristic function of \(\mathcal N(0,\sigma^2 I)\) at \(2\pi(x-x')\) (Rahimi and Recht, 2007):

$$
\frac1m\,\mathbb E\big[\gamma(x)\cdot\gamma(x')\big]=e^{-2\pi^2\sigma^2\lVert x-x'\rVert^2}.
$$
\alttext(One over m times the expected dot product of gamma x and gamma x prime equals e to the minus 2 pi squared sigma squared times the squared distance between x and x prime.)

In the neural tangent kernel view, an MLP on \(\gamma(x)\) behaves like kernel regression with a kernel built on this one (Tancik et al., 2020). So \(\sigma\) sets the bandwidth. The kernel falls to one half at distance \(\sqrt{\ln2/2}/(\pi\sigma)\approx0.187/\sigma\). If that is much larger than the detail in the data, the fit is blurry. If it is smaller than the spacing of the training points, the model has no basis for interpolating between them.

> Simple examples

>> Tables, learned rows, RoPE, scaling, 2-D tables and ALiBi

The program prints the \(d=16\) sinusoidal table and checks its norms, its rotations and its dot products. It adds a learned table of 10 rows to a sequence of length 6, compares RoPE with a hand rotation and evaluates the score of one query and key at many position pairs, including fractional ones. It tabulates the RoPE rates for a 4-fold extension, checks the separability of the 2-D table and prints ALiBi's attention for 4 heads.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/positional_tables.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
using dm::graphics::RGB;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;

double max_diff(const Tensor& a, const Tensor& b) { return nn::max(nn::abs(a - b)).item(); }

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  // 1. The sinusoidal table: wavelengths, a few rows, row norms, shifts as rotations, offset-only dot products.
  const std::int64_t T = 64, d = 16;
  const Tensor pe = nn::sinusoidal_encoding(T, d, 10000.0, F64);
  std::print("Sinusoidal table, d = 16. Wavelengths 2π·10000^(2i/d):");
  for (int i = 0; i < d / 2; ++i) std::print(" {:.1f}", 2 * M_PI * std::pow(10000.0, 2.0 * i / d));
  std::print("\n\n| position | sin₀ | cos₀ | sin₁ | cos₁ | sin₂ | cos₂ | … | sin₇ | cos₇ |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int t : {0, 1, 2, 3, 10, 50}) {
    std::print("| {} |", t);
    for (int j = 0; j < 6; ++j) std::print(" {:.3f} |", pe.at({t, j}));
    std::print(" … | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |\n", pe.at({t, 14}), pe.at({t, 15}));
  }
  const Tensor norms = nn::sqrt(nn::sum(nn::square(pe), {1}));
  std::print("\n‖PE_t‖ ranges over [{:.6f}, {:.6f}]; √(d/2) = {:.6f}.\n", nn::min(norms).item(), nn::max(norms).item(), std::sqrt(d / 2.0));
  double err = 0;
  for (int k : {1, 5, 20})
    for (int t = 0; t + k < T; ++t)
      for (int i = 0; i < d / 2; ++i) {
        const double th = k * std::pow(10000.0, -2.0 * i / d), s = pe.at({t, 2 * i}), c = pe.at({t, 2 * i + 1});
        err = std::max({err, std::abs(s * std::cos(th) + c * std::sin(th) - pe.at({t + k, 2 * i})), std::abs(-s * std::sin(th) + c * std::cos(th) - pe.at({t + k, 2 * i + 1}))});
      }
  double derr = 0;
  for (int t = 0; t < 40; ++t)
    for (int k = 0; k < 24; ++k) {
      double dot = 0, closed = 0;
      for (int j = 0; j < d; ++j) dot += pe.at({t, j}) * pe.at({t + k, j});
      for (int i = 0; i < d / 2; ++i) closed += std::cos(k * std::pow(10000.0, -2.0 * i / d));
      derr = std::max(derr, std::abs(dot - closed));
    }
  std::print("max |PE_(t+k) − R_k PE_t| for k = 1, 5, 20 = {:.1e}; max |PE_t · PE_(t+k) − Σ_i cos(k ω_i)| over t < 40, k < 24 = {:.1e}.\n", err, derr);

  // 2. A learned table [10, 4] added to a sequence of length 6: gradient reaches only rows 0-5.
  nn::ParameterStore p(1, F64);
  nn::Generator gen(1);
  const Tensor x = nn::normal({2, 6, 4}, 0.0, 1.0, gen, F64);
  const Tensor table = nn::learned_encoding(p, "pos", 10, 4);
  const Tensor tab = p.at("pos/pos");
  const Tensor y = x + nn::slice(table, {0, 0}, {6, 4});
  const Tensor gt = nn::grad(nn::sum(nn::square(y)), {tab})[0];
  std::print("\nLearned table [10, 4] on a sequence of length 6, gradient row norms:");
  for (std::int64_t t = 0; t < 10; ++t) std::print(" {:.2f}", std::sqrt(nn::sum(nn::square(nn::slice(gt, {t, 0}, {1, 4}))).item()));
  std::print(".\n");

  // 3. RoPE: against a hand rotation, norms, and scores that depend on the offset only.
  const std::int64_t Tr = 6, dr = 8;
  const Tensor q = nn::normal({1, Tr, dr}, 0.0, 1.0, gen, F64), kk = nn::normal({1, Tr, dr}, 0.0, 1.0, gen, F64);
  const Tensor rq = nn::apply_rotary(q);
  double rerr = 0;
  for (std::int64_t t = 0; t < Tr; ++t)
    for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < dr / 2; ++j) {
      const double a = t * std::pow(10000.0, -2.0 * j / dr), u = q.at({0, t, 2 * j}), v = q.at({0, t, 2 * j + 1});
      rerr = std::max({rerr, std::abs(u * std::cos(a) - v * std::sin(a) - rq.at({0, t, 2 * j})), std::abs(u * std::sin(a) + v * std::cos(a) - rq.at({0, t, 2 * j + 1}))});
    }
  std::print("\nRoPE on q [1, 6, 8]: max |RoPE(q) − hand rotation| = {:.1e}; max |‖q_t‖² − ‖RoPE(q)_t‖²| = {:.1e}.\n", rerr,
             nn::max(nn::abs(nn::sum(nn::square(q), {2}) - nn::sum(nn::square(rq), {2}))).item());
  // One q vector and one k vector placed at many position pairs.
  const Tensor q0 = nn::slice(q, {0, 0, 0}, {1, 1, dr}), k0 = nn::slice(kk, {0, 0, 0}, {1, 1, dr});
  const auto th = nn::rope_inverse_frequencies(dr);
  auto score = [&](double m, double n) { return nn::sum(nn::apply_rotary_at(q0, {m}, th) * nn::apply_rotary_at(k0, {n}, th)).item(); };
  std::print("\n| offset m − n | score at n = 0 | n = 5 | n = 17 | n = 2.5 |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int off : {0, 1, 3, 10})
    std::print("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |\n", off, score(off, 0), score(off + 5, 5), score(off + 17, 17), score(off + 2.5, 2.5));

  // 4. RoPE scaling for a context 4 times longer: the rotation rates of each pair.
  const auto none = nn::rope_inverse_frequencies(dr), pi = nn::rope_inverse_frequencies(dr, 10000.0, nn::RopeScaling::PositionInterpolation, 4.0),
             ntk = nn::rope_inverse_frequencies(dr, 10000.0, nn::RopeScaling::Ntk, 4.0);
  std::print("\n| pair j | θ_j | position interpolation, s = 4 | NTK-aware, s = 4 | wavelength 2π/θ_j |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int j = 0; j < dr / 2; ++j) std::print("| {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.1f} |\n", j, none[j], pi[j], ntk[j], 2 * M_PI / none[j]);

  // 5. The 2-D table for a 4 × 6 grid of patches, d = 16: dot products are a row-offset term plus a column-offset term.
  const Tensor t2 = nn::sinusoidal_encoding_2d(4, 6, 16);
  const Tensor dots = nn::matmul(t2, t2, false, true);
  double serr = 0;
  const Tensor r8 = nn::sinusoidal_encoding(4, 8, 10000.0, F64), c8 = nn::sinusoidal_encoding(6, 8, 10000.0, F64);
  for (int a = 0; a < 24; ++a)
    for (int b = 0; b < 24; ++b) {
      double rr = 0, cc = 0;
      for (int j = 0; j < 8; ++j) rr += r8.at({a / 6, j}) * r8.at({b / 6, j}), cc += c8.at({a % 6, j}) * c8.at({b % 6, j});
      serr = std::max(serr, std::abs(dots.at({a, b}) - rr - cc));
    }
  std::print("\n2-D table for a 4 × 6 grid, d = 16: shape [{}, {}]; max |PE(r, c) · PE(r′, c′) − (row term + column term)| = {:.1e}.\n", t2.size(0), t2.size(1), serr);

  // 6. ALiBi: slopes, the bias of head 0, and the attention it alone induces from the last query.
  const int heads = 4, Ta = 8;
  const auto slopes = nn::alibi_slopes(heads);
  const Tensor bias = nn::alibi_bias(heads, Ta, true, F64);
  const Tensor w = nn::softmax(bias, -1);
  std::print("\nALiBi, 4 heads: slopes");
  for (double s : slopes) std::print(" {:.4f}", s);
  std::print(".\n\n| head (slope) | weights of the last query on keys 0 … 7 |\n| :--- | :--- |\n");
  for (int h = 0; h < heads; ++h) {
    std::print("| {} ({:.4f}) |", h, slopes[h]);
    for (int n = 0; n < Ta; ++n) std::print(" {:.3f}", w.at({h, Ta - 1, n}));
    std::print(" |\n");
  }

  // Figure: the table, and dot products against the offset.
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  {
    const std::int64_t TT = 96, dd = 32;
    const Tensor big = nn::sinusoidal_encoding(TT, dd, 10000.0, F64);
    std::vector<double> xs(dd), ys(TT);
    std::iota(xs.begin(), xs.end(), 0.0);
    std::iota(ys.begin(), ys.end(), 0.0);
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> z(dd, std::vector<double>(TT));
    for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < dd; ++j)
      for (std::int64_t t = 0; t < TT; ++t) z[j][t] = big.at({t, j});
    std::vector<std::string> ylab;
    for (std::int64_t t = 0; t < TT; ++t) ylab.push_back("");
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    pl.image(xs, ys, z, dm::graphics::diverging_colors(33), -1.0, 1.0).x_limits(-0.5, dd - 0.5).y_limits(-0.5, TT - 0.5).title("Sinusoidal table, d = 32")
        .x_label("column (sin, cos pairs; wavelength grows to the right)").y_label("position t");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    std::vector<double> kx, closed;
    for (double k = 0; k <= 48; k += 0.25) {
      double c = 0;
      for (int i = 0; i < d / 2; ++i) c += std::cos(k * std::pow(10000.0, -2.0 * i / d));
      kx.push_back(k), closed.push_back(c);
    }
    pl.lines(kx, closed, "Σ_i cos(k ω_i)", RGB{148, 163, 184}, 3);
    const std::vector<RGB> cols{{37, 99, 235}, {234, 88, 12}};
    int ci = 0;
    for (int t : {0, 15}) {
      std::vector<double> ks, ds;
      for (int k = 0; k <= 48 && t + k < T; k += 2) {
        double dot = 0;
        for (int j = 0; j < d; ++j) dot += pe.at({t, j}) * pe.at({t + k, j});
        ks.push_back(k), ds.push_back(dot);
      }
      pl.points(ks, ds, std::format("from position {}", t), cols[ci], ci == 0 ? 9 : 4.5);
      ++ci;
    }
    pl.x_limits(0, 48).y_limits(0, 11).title("PE_t · PE_(t+k), d = 16").x_label("offset k").y_label("dot product");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  fig.size(1100, 480).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/positional_tables.svg");
}
```
