// result_end

\caption(Left: the sinusoidal table for d = 32 and positions 0 to 95; columns are sin, cos pairs whose wavelength grows to the right. Right: the dot product of positions t and t + k for d = 16, starting from position 0 (large blue points) and from position 15 (small orange points), with the closed form as a grey line.)
\alttext(Left: the leftmost columns alternate red and blue rapidly down the rows, columns further right change more and more slowly, and the rightmost columns are constant red (sine near 0, cosine near 1). Right: the grey curve starts at 8 and oscillates downwards to about 2.5 at offset 48; every orange point sits exactly in the centre of a blue point, and all of them lie on the curve.)

Every row of the table has norm 2.828427, which is \(\sqrt{8}\). The rotation \(R_k\) reproduces shifted rows to \(3\times10^{-15}\), and the dot products match \(\sum_i\cos(k\omega_i)\) to \(4\times10^{-15}\) wherever they start, as the coinciding points in the figure show. The dot product decays only slowly and unevenly with distance, so the sinusoidal table itself is a weak recency signal. The learned table's gradient reaches rows 0–5 and is exactly zero for rows 6–9.

RoPE agrees exactly with a hand rotation and preserves norms to \(9\times10^{-16}\). The same query and key give the same score for a given offset whether the key sits at 0, 5, 17 or 2.5: 2.8596 at offset 0, 3.0086 at 1, 2.4817 at 3 and 1.4113 at 10. The scaling table shows the difference between the two extension rules. Position interpolation divides every rate by 4. NTK-aware scaling leaves the fastest pair at 1, divides the slowest by exactly 4 and the ones in between by less (0.063 against 0.025 for pair 1).
The 2-D table's dot products equal a row term plus a column term to \(2\times10^{-15}\). ALiBi's steepest head (slope 1/4) puts 0.256 of the last query's attention on the current token and 0.044 on the token 7 back. The flattest head (slope 1/256) is almost uniform, between 0.123 and 0.127.

> Checking the theory

>> Feature kernels, RoPE decay, ALiBi span and context scaling

The program estimates the Gaussian-feature kernel with \(m=4096\) features for \(\sigma=1,3,10\) and compares it with \(e^{-2\pi^2\sigma^2\Delta^2}\). It also checks the NeRF kernel against \(\sum_k\cos(2^k\pi\Delta)\). It evaluates Su et al.'s bound for \(d=128\) and tests it on 200 random query–key pairs at 45 distances up to 4000. It compares ALiBi's mean attended distance for 16 heads and \(T=1024\) with the closed form, and computes the RoPE wavelengths for \(d=64\) under both extension rules, with position interpolation checked at scaled positions.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/positional_theory.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
using dm::graphics::RGB;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const RGB blue{37, 99, 235}, orange{234, 88, 12}, green{22, 163, 74}, purple{147, 51, 234}, grey{148, 163, 184}, black{30, 30, 30};
  nn::Generator gen(3);
  // 1. Gaussian Fourier features: γ(x)·γ(x')/m against the Gaussian kernel exp(−2π²σ²Δ²), 1-D, m = 4096.
  const std::int64_t m = 4096;
  std::vector<double> dx;
  for (double u = 0; u <= 0.5001; u += 0.005) dx.push_back(u);
  const Tensor xs = nn::reshape(nn::from_vector(dx, {static_cast<std::int64_t>(dx.size())}), {-1, 1});
  const std::vector<double> sigmas{1.0, 3.0, 10.0};
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> kern(sigmas.size());
  std::print("| σ | max |empirical − exp(−2π²σ²Δ²)| over Δ ∈ [0, 0.5], m = 4096 | 1/√m, for scale |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t s = 0; s < sigmas.size(); ++s) {
    const Tensor B = nn::normal({1, m}, 0.0, sigmas[s], gen, F64);
    const Tensor g = nn::gaussian_fourier_features(xs, B);
    const Tensor k0 = nn::matmul(g, nn::slice(g, {0, 0}, {1, 2 * m}), false, true) / static_cast<double>(m);  // kernel against x' = 0
    double err = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < dx.size(); ++i) {
      kern[s].push_back(k0.at({static_cast<std::int64_t>(i), 0}));
      err = std::max(err, std::abs(kern[s].back() - std::exp(-2 * M_PI * M_PI * sigmas[s] * sigmas[s] * dx[i] * dx[i])));
    }
    std::print("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |\n", sigmas[s], err, 1 / std::sqrt(double(m)));
  }
  // NeRF octaves: the kernel of fourier_features without the input is Σ_k cos(2^k π Δ), which is not stationary-Gaussian.
  const Tensor nf = nn::fourier_features(xs, 6, false);
  double nerr = 0;
  std::vector<double> nerf_k;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < dx.size(); ++i) {
    double dot = 0, closed = 0;
    for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < nf.size(1); ++j) dot += nf.at({static_cast<std::int64_t>(i), j}) * nf.at({0, j});
    for (int k = 0; k < 6; ++k) closed += std::cos(std::ldexp(1.0, k) * M_PI * dx[i]);
    nerf_k.push_back(dot / 6), nerr = std::max(nerr, std::abs(dot - closed));
  }
  std::print("NeRF encoding with 6 octaves: max |γ(x)·γ(0) − Σ_k cos(2^k π Δ)| = {:.1e}.\n", nerr);

  // 2. RoPE long-range decay (Su et al. 2021). Write q_m·k_n = Re Σ_j h_j e^{i r θ_j} with h_j = (q_{2j} + i q_{2j+1}) conj(k_{2j} + i k_{2j+1})
  //    and r = m − n. Abel summation with S_j = Σ_{l<j} e^{i r θ_l} gives |q_m·k_n| ≤ max_j |h_{j+1} − h_j| Σ_{j=1}^{d/2} |S_j| (h_{d/2} = 0).
  const std::int64_t d = 128;
  const auto th = nn::rope_inverse_frequencies(d);
  std::vector<double> rr, bound_avg;
  for (double e = 0; e <= 3.61; e += 0.02) {
    const double r = std::round(std::pow(10.0, e));
    if (!rr.empty() && r == rr.back()) continue;
    std::complex<double> S = 0;
    double tot = 0;
    for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < d / 2; ++j) S += std::polar(1.0, r * th[j]), tot += std::abs(S);
    rr.push_back(r), bound_avg.push_back(tot / (d / 2));
  }
  double worst_ratio = 0;
  std::vector<double> sx, sy;
  const Tensor Q = nn::normal({200, d}, 0.0, 1.0, gen, F64), K = nn::normal({200, d}, 0.0, 1.0, gen, F64);
  for (int t = 0; t < 200; ++t) {
    for (int ri = 0; ri < static_cast<int>(rr.size()); ri += 3) {
      const double r = rr[ri];
      const Tensor qr = nn::apply_rotary_at(nn::slice(Q, {t, 0}, {1, d}), {r}, th);
      const double score = nn::sum(qr * nn::slice(K, {t, 0}, {1, d})).item();
      double hmax = 0;
      std::complex<double> prev = 0;
      for (std::int64_t j = d / 2 - 1; j >= -1; --j) {  // max |h_{j+1} − h_j| including h_{d/2} = 0
        const std::complex<double> h = j >= 0 ? std::complex<double>(Q.at({t, 2 * j}), Q.at({t, 2 * j + 1})) * std::conj(std::complex<double>(K.at({t, 2 * j}), K.at({t, 2 * j + 1}))) : 0.0;
        if (j < d / 2 - 1 || true) hmax = std::max(hmax, std::abs(h - prev));
        prev = h;
        if (j < 0) break;
      }
      const double bound = hmax * bound_avg[ri] * (d / 2);
      worst_ratio = std::max(worst_ratio, std::abs(score) / bound);
      if (t < 20) sx.push_back(std::log10(r)), sy.push_back(std::abs(score) / (hmax * (d / 2)));
    }
  }
  std::print("\nRoPE, d = 128: (1/(d/2)) Σ_j |S_j| is {:.3f} at r = 1, {:.3f} at r = 10, {:.3f} at r = 100, {:.3f} at r = 1000.\n", bound_avg[0],
             bound_avg[std::ranges::find(rr, 10.0) - rr.begin()], bound_avg[std::ranges::find(rr, 100.0) - rr.begin()], bound_avg[std::ranges::find(rr, 1000.0) - rr.begin()]);
  std::print("For 200 random (q, k) pairs at {} offsets, the largest |q_m·k_n| / bound is {:.3f} (the bound holds when ≤ 1).\n", (rr.size() + 2) / 3, worst_ratio);

  // 3. ALiBi: with equal content scores the last query's weights are ∝ e^{−s k} for distance k = 0 … T−1.
  //    Mean attended distance: Σ k e^{−sk} / Σ e^{−sk}; for T → ∞ it is 1/(e^s − 1).
  const int Ta = 1024;
  const auto slopes = nn::alibi_slopes(16);
  const Tensor w = nn::softmax(nn::alibi_bias(16, Ta, true, F64), -1);
  std::vector<double> ls, md, mclosed;
  double aerr = 0;
  std::print("\n| head | slope s | mean distance from softmax | Σ k e^{{−sk}} / Σ e^{{−sk}} | 1/(e^s − 1) |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int h = 0; h < 16; ++h) {
    double mean = 0, num = 0, den = 0;
    for (int n = 0; n < Ta; ++n) mean += (Ta - 1 - n) * w.at({h, Ta - 1, n});
    for (int k = 0; k < Ta; ++k) num += k * std::exp(-slopes[h] * k), den += std::exp(-slopes[h] * k);
    aerr = std::max(aerr, std::abs(mean - num / den));
    ls.push_back(std::log2(slopes[h])), md.push_back(std::log10(mean)), mclosed.push_back(1 / std::expm1(slopes[h]));
    if (h % 3 == 0 || h == 15) std::print("| {} | {:.6f} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} |\n", h, slopes[h], mean, num / den, mclosed.back());
  }
  std::print("Largest difference between the softmax and the finite closed form over all 16 heads: {:.1e}.\n", aerr);

  // 4. RoPE scaling: wavelength 2π/θ_j of each pair, for a model trained on L = 2048 and extended 4 times.
  const std::int64_t dd = 64, L = 2048;
  const auto none = nn::rope_inverse_frequencies(dd), pi = nn::rope_inverse_frequencies(dd, 10000.0, nn::RopeScaling::PositionInterpolation, 4.0),
             ntk = nn::rope_inverse_frequencies(dd, 10000.0, nn::RopeScaling::Ntk, 4.0);
  int full_turns = 0;
  for (double t : none) full_turns += 2 * M_PI / t <= L;
  std::print("\nd = 64, trained on L = 2048: {} of 32 pairs complete a full turn within L. Position interpolation divides every rate by 4 (fastest pair: angle per token {:.3f} instead of 1); "
             "NTK-aware scaling keeps the fastest pair ({:.3f}) and divides the slowest by {:.3f}.\n", full_turns, pi[0], ntk[0], none[31] / ntk[31]);
  // A sanity check of what position interpolation promises: scores at positions (4m, 4n) with PI equal those at (m, n) without.
  const Tensor q = nn::normal({1, dd}, 0.0, 1.0, gen, F64), k = nn::normal({1, dd}, 0.0, 1.0, gen, F64);
  double pierr = 0;
  for (double mpos : {3.0, 100.0, 1500.0})
    for (double npos : {0.0, 77.0, 2000.0}) {
      const double s1 = nn::sum(nn::apply_rotary_at(q, {4 * mpos}, pi) * nn::apply_rotary_at(k, {4 * npos}, pi)).item();
      const double s0 = nn::sum(nn::apply_rotary_at(q, {mpos}, none) * nn::apply_rotary_at(k, {npos}, none)).item();
      pierr = std::max(pierr, std::abs(s1 - s0));
    }
  std::print("Score with interpolation at (4m, 4n) minus score without at (m, n), 9 position pairs: max {:.1e}.\n", pierr);

  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2, 2);
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    const std::vector<RGB> cols{blue, orange, green};
    for (std::size_t s = 0; s < sigmas.size(); ++s) {
      std::vector<double> gx, gy;
      for (double u = 0; u <= 0.5001; u += 0.0025) gx.push_back(u), gy.push_back(std::exp(-2 * M_PI * M_PI * sigmas[s] * sigmas[s] * u * u));
      pl.lines(gx, gy, s == 0 ? "exp(−2π²σ²Δ²)" : "", grey, 3).points(dx, kern[s], std::format("σ = {}", sigmas[s]), cols[s], 3.5);
    }
    pl.lines(dx, nerf_k, "NeRF, L = 6 (÷ 6)", purple, 1.8);
    pl.x_limits(0, 0.5).y_limits(-0.45, 1.5).title("Kernel of the feature map").x_label("distance Δ between coordinates").y_label("γ(x) · γ(x′) / m");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    std::vector<double> lr;
    for (double r : rr) lr.push_back(std::log10(r));
    std::vector<double> bn, ones;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < rr.size(); ++i) {
      double c = 0;
      for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < d / 2; ++j) c += std::cos(rr[i] * th[j]);
      bn.push_back(bound_avg[i] / ((d / 2 + 1) / 2.0)), ones.push_back(std::abs(c) / (d / 2));
    }
    pl.lines(lr, ones, "|score|, q = k = 1", orange, 1.6).lines(lr, bn, "bound Σ|S_j|", blue, 2.4);
    pl.x_limits(0, 3.6).y_limits(0, 1.3).title("RoPE long-range decay, d = 128").x_label("log10 relative distance r").y_label("relative to r = 0");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    std::vector<double> cx, cy, cinf;
    for (double e = -8.6; e <= -0.3; e += 0.05) {
      const double s = std::pow(2.0, e);
      double num = 0, den = 0;
      for (int kk = 0; kk < Ta; ++kk) num += kk * std::exp(-s * kk), den += std::exp(-s * kk);
      cx.push_back(e), cy.push_back(std::log10(num / den)), cinf.push_back(std::log10(1 / std::expm1(s)));
    }
    pl.lines(cx, cinf, "1/(e^s − 1), T = ∞", grey, 1.5).lines(cx, cy, "closed form, T = 1024", black, 1.5).points(ls, md, "alibi_bias, 16 heads", orange, 6);
    pl.x_limits(-9, 0).y_limits(-0.3, 3.4).title("ALiBi: mean attended distance").x_label("log2 slope").y_label("log10 mean distance (keys back)");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    std::vector<double> jx, w0, w1, w2;
    for (int j = 0; j < 32; ++j) jx.push_back(j), w0.push_back(std::log10(2 * M_PI / none[j])), w1.push_back(std::log10(2 * M_PI / pi[j])), w2.push_back(std::log10(2 * M_PI / ntk[j]));
    pl.abline_h(std::log10(double(L)), black, 1).abline_h(std::log10(4.0 * L), grey, 1);
    pl.points(jx, w0, "no scaling", blue, 5).points(jx, w1, "interpolation ×4", orange, 5).points(jx, w2, "NTK-aware ×4", green, 5);
    pl.text(5, std::log10(double(L)) + 0.17, "L = 2048").text(5, std::log10(4.0 * L) + 0.17, "4L");
    pl.x_limits(-1, 32).y_limits(0, 6.5).title("RoPE wavelengths for a 4× longer context").x_label("pair j").y_label("log10 wavelength 2π/θ_j (tokens)");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  fig.size(1100, 1000).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/positional_theory.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| σ | max |empirical − exp(−2π²σ²Δ²)| over Δ ∈ [0, 0.5], m = 4096 | 1/√m, for scale |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 0.0083 | 0.0156 |
| 3 | 0.0098 | 0.0156 |
| 10 | 0.0195 | 0.0156 |
NeRF encoding with 6 octaves: max |γ(x)·γ(0) − Σ_k cos(2^k π Δ)| = 0.0e+00.

RoPE, d = 128: (1/(d/2)) Σ_j |S_j| is 31.538 at r = 1, 17.954 at r = 10, 10.227 at r = 100, 4.471 at r = 1000.
For 200 random (q, k) pairs at 45 offsets, the largest |q_m·k_n| / bound is 0.039 (the bound holds when ≤ 1).

| head | slope s | mean distance from softmax | Σ k e^{−sk} / Σ e^{−sk} | 1/(e^s − 1) |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 0.707107 | 0.97 | 0.97 | 0.97 |
| 3 | 0.250000 | 3.52 | 3.52 | 3.52 |
| 6 | 0.088388 | 10.82 | 10.82 | 10.82 |
| 9 | 0.031250 | 31.50 | 31.50 | 31.50 |
| 12 | 0.011049 | 90.00 | 90.00 | 90.01 |
| 15 | 0.003906 | 236.40 | 236.40 | 255.50 |
Largest difference between the softmax and the finite closed form over all 16 heads: 2.8e-13.

d = 64, trained on L = 2048: 21 of 32 pairs complete a full turn within L. Position interpolation divides every rate by 4 (fastest pair: angle per token 0.250 instead of 1); NTK-aware scaling keeps the fastest pair (1.000) and divides the slowest by 4.000.
Score with interpolation at (4m, 4n) minus score without at (m, n), 9 position pairs: max 0.0e+00.
\image(figures/positional_theory.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Top left: the kernel of Gaussian Fourier features for three bandwidths (points) against exp(−2π²σ²Δ²) (grey), and the NeRF octave kernel (purple). Top right: Su et al.'s bound and the exact score for q = k = 1, both relative to their value at distance 0. Bottom left: ALiBi's mean attended distance for 16 heads against the slope, with the finite and infinite closed forms. Bottom right: the wavelength of each RoPE pair without scaling and with both 4-fold extension rules, and the training length L and 4L.)
\alttext(Top left: blue, orange and green points follow grey bell curves that are wide, medium and narrow; the purple NeRF curve drops to about 0.4 and then oscillates between minus 0.35 and 0.5. Top right: both curves start near 1 at distance 1 and fall roughly linearly in the logarithm of the distance, to below 0.25 by 4000; the exact score lies above the bound at medium distances, which is allowed because each is scaled by its own value at 0. Bottom left: orange points fall along a straight line from about 240 tokens at the smallest slope to 1 token at the largest; the black finite-T curve bends below the grey infinite-T line only at the smallest slope. Bottom right: three rising rows of points; the orange interpolation row lies a constant 0.6 above the blue row, and the green NTK row starts on the blue row at pair 0 and ends on the orange row at pair 31.)

The estimated Gaussian-feature kernels deviate from \(e^{-2\pi^2\sigma^2\Delta^2}\) by at most 0.008, 0.010 and 0.020. That is the Monte Carlo error of 4096 random features, of order \(1/\sqrt{4096}=0.016\). The NeRF kernel equals \(\sum_k\cos(2^k\pi\Delta)\) exactly. It is not a bump, and it has large side lobes at distances where it should be small.

Su et al.'s bound decays with distance. Relative to distance 0, \(\sum_j|S_j|\) is 0.97 at \(r=1\), 0.55 at 10, 0.31 at 100 and 0.14 at 1000. For random queries and keys the bound holds with a large margin: the largest ratio of \(|q\cdot k|\) to the bound is 0.039, because random \(h_j\) make \(\max_j|h_{j+1}-h_j|\) large. The exact score for \(q=k=1\) decays in the same way, which is the recency preference the bound describes.

ALiBi's mean attended distance agrees with the finite closed form to \(2.8\times10^{-13}\) for all 16 heads. It runs from 0.97 tokens for the steepest head to 236 for the flattest, close to \(1/(e^s-1)\) except for the flattest head, whose span (255.5 for unlimited context) is cut short by \(T=1024\).

For \(d=64\) and \(L=2048\), 21 of the 32 RoPE pairs complete a full turn within the training length. The other 11 are the ones that see new angles at longer contexts. Position interpolation shifts all wavelengths up by a factor of 4. NTK-aware scaling keeps the fastest pair's angle per token at 1.000 and multiplies the slowest pair's wavelength by exactly 4. With position interpolation, scores at positions \((4m,4n)\) equal the original scores at \((m,n)\) exactly for all 9 pairs tested.

> A dataset: a coordinate MLP on the volcano

The volcano data (R Core Team, 2024) is an \(87\times61\) grid of elevations of Maunga Whau in Auckland, in metres. Coordinates are scaled by the longer side to \([0,1]\times[0,0.70]\) and elevation is standardised. Every other row and column, 1364 points, trains, and the other 3943 points test. This asks the model to interpolate at half the training spacing.
The MLP has four hidden ReLU layers of width 64, in single precision, trained by Adam (step \(3\times10^{-3}\)) for 200 full-batch steps, for two seeds. The inputs are the raw coordinates, NeRF octaves with \(L=4\) or 6, or 128 Gaussian Fourier features with \(\sigma=1,4,10,30\). Bilinear interpolation of the training grid is the baseline.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/positional_volcano.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
namespace init = dm::nn::init;
using nn::Activation;
using nn::Tensor;
using dm::graphics::RGB;
const auto F32 = nn::DType::Float32;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  // The volcano elevation grid (R's datasets::volcano), long format x, y, elevation.
  const auto df = dm::datasets::volcano();
  const auto xv = df.get_column<double>("x").values(), yv = df.get_column<double>("y").values(), ev = df.get_column<double>("elevation").values();
  const double xmax = *std::ranges::max_element(xv), ymax = *std::ranges::max_element(yv);
  const double step = xv[1] - xv[0];
  const auto nx = static_cast<std::int64_t>(std::llround(xmax / step)) + 1, ny = static_cast<std::int64_t>(std::llround(ymax / step)) + 1;
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> grid(nx, std::vector<double>(ny));
  double emean = 0;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < ev.size(); ++i) grid[std::llround(xv[i] / step)][std::llround(yv[i] / step)] = ev[i], emean += ev[i] / ev.size();
  double evar = 0;
  for (double e : ev) evar += (e - emean) * (e - emean) / ev.size();
  const double esd = std::sqrt(evar);
  std::print("Volcano grid: {} × {} = {} points, elevation {} to {} m (mean {:.1f}, sd {:.1f}).\n", nx, ny, ev.size(), *std::ranges::min_element(ev), *std::ranges::max_element(ev), emean, esd);
  // Coordinates scaled by the longer side so that both axes have the same unit; train on every other point in each direction.
  const double scale = static_cast<double>(std::max(nx, ny) - 1);
  std::vector<double> ctr, cte, ttr, tte, call, tall;
  for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < nx; ++i)
    for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < ny; ++j) {
      const double u = i / scale, v = j / scale, t = (grid[i][j] - emean) / esd;
      call.insert(call.end(), {u, v}), tall.push_back(t);
      if (i % 2 == 0 && j % 2 == 0) ctr.insert(ctr.end(), {u, v}), ttr.push_back(t);
      else cte.insert(cte.end(), {u, v}), tte.push_back(t);
    }
  const auto ntr = static_cast<std::int64_t>(ttr.size()), nte = static_cast<std::int64_t>(tte.size()), nall = static_cast<std::int64_t>(tall.size());
  const Tensor Ctr = nn::from_vector(ctr, {ntr, 2}).to(F32), Cte = nn::from_vector(cte, {nte, 2}).to(F32), Call = nn::from_vector(call, {nall, 2}).to(F32);
  const Tensor Ytr = nn::from_vector(ttr, {ntr, 1}).to(F32), Yte = nn::from_vector(tte, {nte, 1}).to(F32);
  std::print("Training on {} points (every other row and column), testing on the other {}.\n\n", ntr, nte);

  struct Enc { std::string label; int kind; double param; };  // kind 0 raw, 1 NeRF octaves, 2 Gaussian features
  const std::vector<Enc> encs{{"raw (x, y)", 0, 0}, {"NeRF octaves, L = 4", 1, 4}, {"NeRF octaves, L = 6", 1, 6}, {"Gaussian, σ = 1", 2, 1},
                              {"Gaussian, σ = 4", 2, 4}, {"Gaussian, σ = 10", 2, 10}, {"Gaussian, σ = 30", 2, 30}};
  const int steps = 200;
  std::print("| encoding | inputs | train RMSE (m) | test RMSE (m) |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> recon(encs.size());
  std::vector<double> test_rmse_mean;
  for (std::size_t e = 0; e < encs.size(); ++e) {
    std::vector<double> tr_r, te_r;
    std::int64_t width = 0;
    for (std::uint64_t seed : {1, 2}) {
      nn::Generator g(100 + seed);
      const Tensor B = nn::normal({2, 128}, 0.0, encs[e].param > 0 ? encs[e].param : 1.0, g, F32);
      auto encode = [&](const Tensor& c) {
        if (encs[e].kind == 0) return c;
        if (encs[e].kind == 1) return nn::fourier_features(c, static_cast<std::int64_t>(encs[e].param));
        return nn::gaussian_fourier_features(c, B);
      };
      nn::ParameterStore p(seed, F32);
      const nn::DenseOptions hid{.activation = Activation::Relu, .init = init::he_normal()};
      auto f = [&](const Tensor& c) {
        Tensor h = encode(c);
        for (int l = 0; l < 4; ++l) h = nn::dense(p, std::format("h{}", l), h, 64, hid);
        return nn::dense(p, "out", h, 1);
      };
      width = encode(nn::slice(Ctr, {0, 0}, {1, 2})).size(1);
      (void)f(nn::slice(Ctr, {0, 0}, {2, 2}));
      nn::Optimizer opt(p, 3e-3, {.kind = nn::OptimizerKind::Adam});
      for (int s = 0; s < steps; ++s) opt.minimize(nn::mean(nn::square(f(Ctr) - Ytr)));
      nn::NoGradGuard ng;
      tr_r.push_back(esd * std::sqrt(nn::mean(nn::square(f(Ctr) - Ytr)).item()));
      te_r.push_back(esd * std::sqrt(nn::mean(nn::square(f(Cte) - Yte)).item()));
      if (seed == 1) recon[e] = f(Call).to(nn::DType::Float64).to_vector<double>();
    }
    test_rmse_mean.push_back((te_r[0] + te_r[1]) / 2);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.2f}, {:.2f} | {:.2f}, {:.2f} |\n", encs[e].label, width, tr_r[0], tr_r[1], te_r[0], te_r[1]);
  }
  // Baseline: bilinear interpolation of the training grid (no learning).
  double bil = 0;
  int cnt = 0;
  for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < nx; ++i)
    for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < ny; ++j) {
      if (i % 2 == 0 && j % 2 == 0) continue;
      const std::int64_t i0 = std::min<std::int64_t>(i / 2 * 2, (nx - 1) / 2 * 2), j0 = std::min<std::int64_t>(j / 2 * 2, (ny - 1) / 2 * 2);
      const std::int64_t i1 = std::min<std::int64_t>(i0 + 2, (nx - 1) / 2 * 2), j1 = std::min<std::int64_t>(j0 + 2, (ny - 1) / 2 * 2);
      const double a = i1 == i0 ? 0 : double(i - i0) / (i1 - i0), b = j1 == j0 ? 0 : double(j - j0) / (j1 - j0);
      const double v = (1 - a) * (1 - b) * grid[i0][j0] + a * (1 - b) * grid[i1][j0] + (1 - a) * b * grid[i0][j1] + a * b * grid[i1][j1];
      bil += (v - grid[i][j]) * (v - grid[i][j]), ++cnt;
    }
  std::print("\nBilinear interpolation of the training points (no learning): test RMSE {:.2f} m.\n", std::sqrt(bil / cnt));
  std::print("Training points are {:.4f} apart in scaled coordinates. Distance at which the Gaussian-feature kernel exp(−2π²σ²Δ²) falls to 1/2, √(ln 2 / 2) / (πσ):", 2.0 / scale);
  for (double sg : {1.0, 4.0, 10.0, 30.0}) std::print(" σ = {}: {:.4f};", sg, std::sqrt(std::log(2.0) / 2) / (M_PI * sg));
  std::print("\n");

  // Figure: truth and four reconstructions, plus test RMSE against the encoding.
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(3, 2);
  auto heat = [&](const std::vector<double>& vals, bool standardised, const std::string& title) {
    std::vector<double> xs(nx), ys(ny);
    std::iota(xs.begin(), xs.end(), 0.0);
    std::iota(ys.begin(), ys.end(), 0.0);
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> z(nx, std::vector<double>(ny));
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < nx; ++i)
      for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < ny; ++j) z[i][j] = standardised ? emean + esd * vals[i * ny + j] : vals[i * ny + j];
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    pl.image(xs, ys, z, dm::graphics::terrain_colors(40), 90.0, 200.0).x_limits(-0.5, nx - 0.5).y_limits(-0.5, ny - 0.5).title(title).x_label("row index").y_label("column index");
    fig.add(pl);
  };
  std::vector<double> truth;
  for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < nx; ++i)
    for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < ny; ++j) truth.push_back(grid[i][j]);
  heat(truth, false, "Volcano elevation (truth)");
  heat(recon[0], true, std::format("Raw coordinates (test RMSE {:.1f} m)", test_rmse_mean[0]));
  heat(recon[2], true, std::format("NeRF octaves, L = 6 ({:.1f} m)", test_rmse_mean[2]));
  heat(recon[5], true, std::format("Gaussian features, σ = 10 ({:.1f} m)", test_rmse_mean[5]));
  heat(recon[6], true, std::format("Gaussian features, σ = 30 ({:.1f} m)", test_rmse_mean[6]));
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    std::vector<double> xs, ys;
    for (std::size_t e = 0; e < encs.size(); ++e) xs.push_back(e), ys.push_back(test_rmse_mean[e]);
    for (double& y : ys) y = std::log10(y);
    pl.points(xs, ys, "MLP, mean of 2 seeds", RGB{37, 99, 235}, 7).abline_h(std::log10(std::sqrt(bil / cnt)), RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.5);
    pl.text(4.6, std::log10(std::sqrt(bil / cnt)) - 0.12, "bilinear interpolation");
    pl.x_limits(-0.5, encs.size() - 0.5).x_tick_labels({"raw", "L=4", "L=6", "σ=1", "σ=4", "σ=10", "σ=30"}).y_limits(-0.5, 2.0)
        .title("Test RMSE by encoding").x_label("encoding").y_label("log10 test RMSE (m)");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  fig.size(1100, 1350).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/positional_volcano.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Volcano grid: 87 × 61 = 5307 points, elevation 94 to 195 m (mean 130.2, sd 25.8).
Training on 1364 points (every other row and column), testing on the other 3943.

| encoding | inputs | train RMSE (m) | test RMSE (m) |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| raw (x, y) | 2 | 4.97, 4.30 | 4.96, 4.26 |
| NeRF octaves, L = 4 | 18 | 0.94, 0.87 | 1.18, 1.20 |
| NeRF octaves, L = 6 | 26 | 0.84, 0.71 | 3.16, 3.18 |
| Gaussian, σ = 1 | 256 | 0.61, 0.65 | 0.86, 0.89 |
| Gaussian, σ = 4 | 256 | 0.01, 0.02 | 2.31, 2.36 |
| Gaussian, σ = 10 | 256 | 0.00, 0.00 | 16.40, 15.49 |
| Gaussian, σ = 30 | 256 | 0.00, 0.00 | 29.74, 29.93 |

Bilinear interpolation of the training points (no learning): test RMSE 0.70 m.
Training points are 0.0233 apart in scaled coordinates. Distance at which the Gaussian-feature kernel exp(−2π²σ²Δ²) falls to 1/2, √(ln 2 / 2) / (πσ): σ = 1: 0.1874; σ = 4: 0.0468; σ = 10: 0.0187; σ = 30: 0.0062;
\image(figures/positional_volcano.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The volcano elevation map and four reconstructions from a quarter of its points (seed 1), with each encoding's mean test RMSE; bottom right: test RMSE (log scale) of every encoding, mean of two seeds, with bilinear interpolation as a grey line.)
\alttext(The raw-coordinate reconstruction looks like a smoothed copy of the truth that has lost the crater. The NeRF L = 6 reconstruction has the right shape with small blocky artefacts. With sigma = 10 the shape is visible but covered in pixel-scale noise, and with sigma = 30 it is mostly noise. Bottom right: log10 RMSE is 0.66 for raw, 0.08 for L = 4, 0.5 for L = 6, minus 0.06 for sigma = 1, 0.37 for sigma = 4, 1.2 for sigma = 10 and 1.47 for sigma = 30; the bilinear line is at minus 0.15.)

The volcano is smooth relative to its grid, which makes it a fair test of interpolation. Raw coordinates show the spectral bias directly: after 200 steps the MLP still misses its own training points by 4.30–4.97 m. Every Fourier encoding drives the training error to 0.94 m or less, and Gaussian features with \(\sigma\ge4\) to 0.02 m or less. Fitting the training points is not the same as interpolating them, and the test error depends on the kernel bandwidth as the theory predicts.
The training points are 0.0233 apart. The Gaussian-feature kernel falls to one half at 0.187 (\(\sigma=1\)), 0.047 (\(\sigma=4\)), 0.019 (\(\sigma=10\)) and 0.006 (\(\sigma=30\)).
With \(\sigma=1\) the test error is lowest (0.86 and 0.89 m) and close to bilinear interpolation (0.70 m). With \(\sigma=4\), about two spacings, it grows to about 2.3 m. Once the half-width drops below one spacing, the model memorises the training points and predicts noise between them: 15–16 m at \(\sigma=10\) and 30 m at \(\sigma=30\), which is worse than predicting the mean (the elevation's standard deviation is 25.8 m).
NeRF octaves with \(L=4\) are nearly as good as \(\sigma=1\) (1.18 and 1.20 m), but \(L=6\) already gives 3.2 m, consistent with the side lobes of its kernel.

> Conclusion

Each positional encoding does what its algebra says, to rounding error. Sinusoidal tables turn shifts into fixed rotations and make dot products functions of the offset alone. Learned tables get gradient only where used. RoPE makes attention scores functions of the offset, also at fractional positions, and its scores obey a decay bound that favours nearby tokens. ALiBi gives each head an exponential recency profile whose span is \(1/(e^s-1)\), cut off by the context length.

To extend RoPE, position interpolation maps a longer context exactly onto the trained angles but slows every pair. NTK-aware scaling leaves the fast pairs that resolve neighbouring tokens alone and interpolates only the slow ones. For continuous coordinates, Fourier features overcome the spectral bias of a plain MLP, but their bandwidth must match the data. On the volcano, a wide kernel came close to bilinear interpolation. A kernel narrower than the training spacing memorised the samples and predicted noise in between, ending up worse than a constant.

